                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/63375392?u=13d3a70ed7dffa4b2304d48381cde4723014ca5e&v=4",
                        "login": "beaumccartney",
                        "resourcePath": "/beaumccartney",
                        "url": "https://github.com/beaumccartney"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
                    "body": "One workflow could be go file-by-file, but then how to figure out what files have todo-type patterns in them?\r\n\r\n`hipatterns` provides a nice way to define what a TODO looks like (or generally a pattern of interest), is it a good idea to decouple that from the actual highlighting?\r\n\r\nThis kind of intersects with project-wide search and maybe replace, but with a nice way to define search patterns and give meaning to what they represent.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">One workflow could be go file-by-file, but then how to figure out what files have todo-type patterns in them?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><code class=\"notranslate\">hipatterns</code> provides a nice way to define what a TODO looks like (or generally a pattern of interest), is it a good idea to decouple that from the actual highlighting?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This kind of intersects with project-wide search and maybe replace, but with a nice way to define search patterns and give meaning to what they represent.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "One workflow could be go file-by-file, but then how to figure out what files have todo-type patterns in them?\nhipatterns provides a nice way to define what a TODO looks like (or generally a pattern of interest), is it a good idea to decouple that from the actual highlighting?\nThis kind of intersects with project-wide search and maybe replace, but with a nice way to define search patterns and give meaning to what they represent.",
